# Trivy ignore list — CVE IDs to suppress, one per line, # for comments.
# https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/#trivyignore
#
# ── This file must EXIST even when it is empty. ──────────────────────────────
#
# .github/workflows/security-scan.yml passes `trivyignores: .trivyignore` to
# aquasecurity/trivy-action. When the file is absent the action does not fall
# back to scanning without it — it fails outright:
#
#     ERROR: cannot find ignorefile '.trivyignore'
#     ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1
#
# and exits BEFORE scanning anything. Measured on the first run of this
# workflow against a branch (PR #2, 2026-08-13): the job log contained zero
# lines of scan output — no CVE table, no "Total:", no report summary. The job
# showed a red X, which reads like "CVEs found" and is in fact "nothing was
# examined".
#
# The job is `continue-on-error: true`, i.e. advisory rather than blocking, so
# the failure never stopped a merge and nothing forced anyone to look at it.
# An advisory gate that cannot produce advice is worth less than no gate: it
# occupies the slot where a working scan would go.
#
# So: deleting this file does not disable the ignore list, it disables the
# SCAN. If there is genuinely nothing to suppress, leave it with only comments,
# which is the state it is in now.
#
# ── Adding an entry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One CVE ID per line. Every entry needs a reason and a review date directly
# above it, because a suppression with no expiry is how a known-vulnerable
# dependency becomes permanent:
#
#   # CVE-2026-12345 — vulnerable path is the SAML handler, which this build
#   # compiles out (-tags nosaml). No upstream fix as of 2026-08-13.
#   # Review: 2026-11-13
#   CVE-2026-12345
#
# Prefer upgrading. `exit-code: '0'` and `continue-on-error: true` mean a
# finding here never blocks anyone, so an entry in this file buys nothing
# except a quieter log — the only reason to add one is to stop a known and
# accepted finding from burying a new one.

# (no suppressions)
